WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.991
A California county reports a backlog of one hundred applications.

2
00:00:04.991 --> 00:00:12.337
Will the backlog rise by ten applications each week, rise by ten percent each week, or rise and then fall after temporary staff arrive?

3
00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:14.568
Those are different mathematical stories.

4
00:00:14.568 --> 00:00:17.779
What evidence would let a policy analyst choose among them?

5
00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:19.713
This is study preparation only.

6
00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:23.083
It awards no score, course credit, or transfer credit.

7
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:30.882
Use the audio to guide written practice, then check institutional rules before treating any outside course or exam as credit.

8
00:00:30.882 --> 00:00:32.916
A function is not just a formula.

9
00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:36.492
It is a rule that gives one output for each allowed input.

10
00:00:36.492 --> 00:00:40.499
You can describe it with words, a table, a graph, or an equation.

11
00:00:40.499 --> 00:00:42.965
Good algebra moves among all four forms.

12
00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:44.136
Start with a table.

13
00:00:44.136 --> 00:00:46.972
In week zero, a permit backlog is one hundred.

14
00:00:46.972 --> 00:00:49.130
In week one, it is one hundred ten.

15
00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.473
In week two, it is one hundred twenty.

16
00:00:51.473 --> 00:00:53.939
In week three, it is one hundred thirty.

17
00:00:53.939 --> 00:00:56.651
The first differences are ten, ten, and ten.

18
00:00:56.651 --> 00:00:59.919
A constant first difference signals a linear pattern.

19
00:00:59.919 --> 00:01:03.741
The spoken rule is: start at one hundred and add ten per week.

20
00:01:03.741 --> 00:01:06.700
The rate of change is ten applications per week.

21
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.994
Now change the table.

22
00:01:07.994 --> 00:01:09.536
Week zero is one hundred.

23
00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.262
Week one is one hundred ten.

24
00:01:11.262 --> 00:01:13.420
Week two is one hundred twenty-one.

25
00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.194
Week three is about one hundred thirty-three.

26
00:01:16.194 --> 00:01:21.989
Here the ratios are constant because each value is one and one tenth times the previous value.

27
00:01:21.989 --> 00:01:25.133
This is exponential growth at ten percent per week.

28
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.291
Linear growth adds the same amount.

29
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:30.311
Exponential growth multiplies by the same factor.

30
00:01:30.311 --> 00:01:34.010
The two models agree after one week and separate after that.

31
00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:38.202
A convenient first data point is not enough to identify the pattern.

32
00:01:38.202 --> 00:01:46.402
Look across several periods, examine differences and ratios, and ask whether a staffing change or capacity limit altered the process.

33
00:01:46.402 --> 00:01:49.114
Function notation helps organize evaluation.

34
00:01:49.114 --> 00:01:54.663
If f of x equals x squared minus four x, then f of three means replace every x with three.

35
00:01:54.663 --> 00:01:56.019
Three squared is nine.

36
00:01:56.019 --> 00:01:57.683
Four times three is twelve.

37
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:59.903
Nine minus twelve is negative three.

38
00:01:59.903 --> 00:02:03.170
Parentheses matter when substituting negative values.

39
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:10.506
For f of negative two, negative two squared is four, while negative four times negative two contributes positive eight.

40
00:02:10.506 --> 00:02:11.801
The output is twelve.

41
00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:15.192
Domain restrictions come from both algebra and context.

42
00:02:15.192 --> 00:02:19.507
A denominator cannot equal zero because division by zero is undefined.

43
00:02:19.507 --> 00:02:24.192
A square root, when working with real numbers, cannot take a negative input.

44
00:02:24.192 --> 00:02:28.138
In a county workload model, time may be nonnegative whole weeks.

45
00:02:28.138 --> 00:02:31.775
A formula can accept a value that the public system cannot.

46
00:02:31.775 --> 00:02:36.565
Polynomial functions combine powers of a variable with numerical coefficients.

47
00:02:36.565 --> 00:02:40.311
A quadratic has degree two, meaning its highest power is two.

48
00:02:40.311 --> 00:02:41.785
Its graph is a parabola.

49
00:02:41.785 --> 00:02:46.145
It can decrease, turn, and increase, so a constant-rate story may fail.

50
00:02:46.145 --> 00:02:49.339
Factoring can reveal where a polynomial equals zero.

51
00:02:49.339 --> 00:02:52.409
Solve x squared minus five x plus six equals zero.

52
00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:56.831
Find two numbers that multiply to positive six and add to negative five.

53
00:02:56.831 --> 00:02:59.349
They are negative two and negative three.

54
00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:02.911
The expression factors as x minus two times x minus three.

55
00:03:02.911 --> 00:03:06.043
A product is zero when at least one factor is zero.

56
00:03:06.043 --> 00:03:08.560
Therefore x equals two or x equals three.

57
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.955
Check: four minus ten plus six is zero.

58
00:03:10.955 --> 00:03:13.473
Nine minus fifteen plus six is also zero.

59
00:03:13.473 --> 00:03:17.281
The quadratic formula handles cases that do not factor easily.

60
00:03:17.281 --> 00:03:23.422
For today, recognize its purpose: it finds solutions to a quadratic equation using the coefficients.

61
00:03:23.422 --> 00:03:29.563
Also recognize that a negative value under its square root means there are no real-number solutions.

62
00:03:29.563 --> 00:03:33.309
Do not force a real answer when the algebra says none exists.

63
00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:36.134
A rational function is a ratio of polynomials.

64
00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:42.460
Consider one hundred divided by x, where x is the number of crews sharing one hundred inspection hours.

65
00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.714
With two crews, the model gives fifty hours per crew.

66
00:03:45.714 --> 00:03:47.372
With five, it gives twenty.

67
00:03:47.372 --> 00:03:48.785
With ten, it gives ten.

68
00:03:48.785 --> 00:03:50.075
But x cannot be zero.

69
00:03:50.075 --> 00:03:56.277
Near zero, the model behaves dramatically, which warns us that a rational relationship is not linear.

70
00:03:56.277 --> 00:03:59.716
Rational equations require checking for excluded values.

71
00:03:59.716 --> 00:04:02.111
Solve twelve divided by x equals three.

72
00:04:02.111 --> 00:04:05.120
Multiply both sides by x, provided x is not zero.

73
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.471
Twelve equals three x.

74
00:04:06.471 --> 00:04:08.559
Divide by three, so x equals four.

75
00:04:08.559 --> 00:04:12.797
Check four in the original equation: twelve divided by four is three.

76
00:04:12.797 --> 00:04:16.113
The check confirms both the arithmetic and the domain.

77
00:04:16.113 --> 00:04:21.149
An inverse function reverses an input-output process when that reversal is unique.

78
00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:25.693
Suppose cost equals fifty plus twenty times the number of service batches.

79
00:04:25.693 --> 00:04:30.360
To recover batches from a known cost, subtract fifty, then divide by twenty.

80
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:36.379
A cost of one hundred fifty gives one hundred after subtraction, then five batches after division.

81
00:04:36.379 --> 00:04:39.756
Not every function has an inverse over its full domain.

82
00:04:39.756 --> 00:04:46.266
Squaring sends both three and negative three to nine, so seeing nine does not reveal which input was used.

83
00:04:46.266 --> 00:04:50.626
Restricting the domain to nonnegative inputs makes the reversal unique.

84
00:04:50.626 --> 00:04:55.969
In public data, the same caution applies: one outcome may have several possible inputs.

85
00:04:55.969 --> 00:04:59.165
Exponential functions model constant percentage change.

86
00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:03.757
Begin with two hundred online requests and increase by five percent each month.

87
00:05:03.757 --> 00:05:05.616
Multiply by one point zero five.

88
00:05:05.616 --> 00:05:08.115
After one month, there are two hundred ten.

89
00:05:08.115 --> 00:05:14.159
After two months, multiply two hundred ten by one point zero five, giving two hundred twenty point five.

90
00:05:14.159 --> 00:05:20.901
A count would need sensible rounding, but keep the decimal during calculation to avoid compounding a rounding error.

91
00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.981
Exponential decay uses a factor between zero and one.

92
00:05:23.981 --> 00:05:28.979
A ten percent monthly reduction leaves ninety percent, so multiply by zero point nine.

93
00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:35.372
A backlog of five hundred becomes four hundred fifty after one month, then four hundred five after two months.

94
00:05:35.372 --> 00:05:40.254
Subtracting fifty each month would be linear decay and would tell a different story.

95
00:05:40.254 --> 00:05:42.636
A logarithm answers an exponent question.

96
00:05:42.636 --> 00:05:48.041
If two raised to the third power equals eight, then the base-two logarithm of eight is three.

97
00:05:48.041 --> 00:05:54.143
In policy forecasting, a logarithm can answer how many compounding periods it takes to cross a threshold.

98
00:05:54.143 --> 00:05:57.166
The base and the growth factor must match the model.

99
00:05:57.166 --> 00:06:01.408
Exponential equations can sometimes be solved by recognizing equal bases.

100
00:06:01.408 --> 00:06:03.733
Solve two raised to x equals thirty-two.

101
00:06:03.733 --> 00:06:07.336
Thirty-two is two raised to the fifth power, so x equals five.

102
00:06:07.336 --> 00:06:11.172
When equal bases are not obvious, logarithms isolate the exponent.

103
00:06:11.172 --> 00:06:18.901
A calculator gives an approximation, but the interpretation remains "the number of growth periods." Sequences describe ordered terms.

104
00:06:18.901 --> 00:06:21.168
An arithmetic sequence adds a constant.

105
00:06:21.168 --> 00:06:26.340
Starting at fifty and adding eight gives fifty, fifty-eight, sixty-six, and seventy-four.

106
00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.013
A geometric sequence multiplies by a constant.

107
00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:34.651
Starting at fifty and multiplying by two gives fifty, one hundred, two hundred, and four hundred.

108
00:06:34.651 --> 00:06:36.859
A series is the sum of sequence terms.

109
00:06:36.859 --> 00:06:38.544
Now apply the models locally.

110
00:06:38.544 --> 00:06:44.879
A California city sees one hundred code-enforcement cases, then one hundred ten, then one hundred twenty-one.

111
00:06:44.879 --> 00:06:47.611
The constant ratio suggests ten percent growth.

112
00:06:47.611 --> 00:06:56.502
Before projecting, ask whether reporting outreach caused a temporary jump, whether duplicate cases exist, and whether staff capacity caps recorded cases.

113
00:06:56.502 --> 00:06:59.583
Mathematics identifies a pattern in observed numbers.

114
00:06:59.583 --> 00:07:01.558
It does not certify the mechanism.

115
00:07:01.558 --> 00:07:04.116
Compare three forecasts for the next period.

116
00:07:04.116 --> 00:07:07.951
A linear rule based on adding ten predicts one hundred thirty-one.

117
00:07:07.951 --> 00:07:14.983
An exponential rule based on multiplying one hundred twenty-one by one point one predicts about one hundred thirty-three.

118
00:07:14.983 --> 00:07:18.819
A capacity rule might cap opened cases at one hundred twenty-five.

119
00:07:18.819 --> 00:07:23.294
Each answer can be calculated correctly while answering a different question.

120
00:07:23.294 --> 00:07:24.281
Three questions.

121
00:07:24.281 --> 00:07:28.353
First, how can a table distinguish linear from exponential change?

122
00:07:28.353 --> 00:07:33.413
Second, why must a rational-function solution be checked in the original equation?

123
00:07:33.413 --> 00:07:36.868
Third, what does a logarithm tell you in a growth model?

124
00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:43.717
First answer: constant differences suggest linear change, while constant nonzero ratios suggest exponential change.

125
00:07:43.717 --> 00:07:50.447
Second answer: algebraic steps may introduce an excluded value, especially a value that makes a denominator zero.

126
00:07:50.447 --> 00:07:53.901
Substitution confirms the candidate is permitted and true.

127
00:07:53.901 --> 00:08:01.882
Third answer: a logarithm gives the exponent, which often represents the number of compounding periods needed to reach a stated value.

128
00:08:01.882 --> 00:08:20.216
Artifact: complete a mixed review of at least twenty problems on function evaluation and domains, linear versus exponential tables, factoring and quadratics, rational expressions, inverses, exponentials, logarithms, sequences, and basic counting or probability.

129
00:08:20.216 --> 00:08:28.084
Earn at least eighty percent, correct every miss in writing, and label the model and units in each word problem.

130
